# Target C-II — paired-remainder energy extends the corrected O--O path through `t<=1.865`

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening  
**Scope:** canonical full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.865`  
**Dependencies:** corrected joint-feasibility retained-mass system; hostile-audited dominant-orbit retained-measure domination and bathtub theorem; retained O--O pair weights; physical Section-157 opposite-branch argument; phase-free O--O path saturation; diameter-path Helly hub  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_paired_remainder_path_t1865.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The corrected high-`t` path argument previously extracted low-energy outer-to-outer trains by charging their pair energy against the entire outer directional-energy budget `delta`. That loses a large amount of information. The same retained dominant occurrence measure already contains substantial **unpaired** occurrence mass, and that residual mass has its own unavoidable bathtub energy.

Keeping that payment yields the exact asymptotic inequality

\[
\boxed{
E_{pair}+F(z-2P)\le\delta+o(1),
\qquad
F(s)=s-6\sin(s/6),
}
\tag{PR.1}
\]

where

- `zD` is retained dominant outer-occurrence mass;
- `PD` is retained dominant O--O pair weight;
- `E_pair D` is its pair-weighted two-terminal directional energy.

This replaces the old coarse bound `E_pair<=delta+o(1)`.

At

\[
\boxed{t\le1.865}
\]

the stronger payment leaves more than `0.011D` retained low-energy O--O pair weight. One exact dominant phase therefore contains more than `0.0036D` actual O--O trains, all with physical opposite terminal branches. Their proper crosscuts form one simple dual path, every path region is diameter-loaded, all actual diameter support intervals share one hub region, and some nonhub adjacent strip has at most `1745` switching diameter rows. Its exact nonzero layer reaction is generated by one or two actual switching diameter bars.

Thus the live corrected path/hub/one-sided-strip normal form jumps from `t<=1.86179` to

\[
\boxed{t\le1.865.}
\tag{PR.2}

This is not decimal tuning: at `t=1.865` the old raw-Markov lower bound is already negative, while the paired-remainder lower bound exceeds `0.011D`.

---

## 1. Pair/remainder decomposition of retained outer occurrences

Freeze the three shrinking phase windows of the dominant exact orbit exactly as in the hostile-audited dominant-orbit outer-energy theorem.

For one dominant train `tau`, let its two retained outer terminal weights be

\[
a_\tau,b_\tau\in[0,1]
\]

and put

\[
w_\tau:=\min(a_\tau,b_\tau).
\tag{PR.3}
\]

Write the exact terminal directional energies as

\[
e_{\tau,1},e_{\tau,2}\ge0.
\]

The normalized retained pair weight and pair energy are

\[
P={1\over D}\sum_\tau w_\tau,
\tag{PR.4}
\]

\[
E_{pair}={1\over D}\sum_\tau
w_\tau(e_{\tau,1}+e_{\tau,2}).
\tag{PR.5}
\]

Subtract weight `w_tau` from both retained occurrences. The residual weights are

\[
a'_\tau=a_\tau-w_\tau,
\qquad
b'_\tau=b_\tau-w_\tau,
\tag{PR.6}
\]

so `a'_tau,b'_tau>=0`, one of them is zero, and their total normalized mass is exactly

\[
\boxed{
{1\over D}\sum_\tau(a'_\tau+b'_\tau)=z-2P.
}
\tag{PR.7}

The original retained occurrence measure is pointwise dominated by the normalized force-hull perimeter measure. Decreasing occurrence weights preserves that domination. Hence the residual measure is also dominated by perimeter measure.

The hostile-audited three-well bathtub theorem therefore applies directly to the residual measure:

\[
E_{res}
:={1\over D}\sum_\tau
(a'_\tau e_{\tau,1}+b'_\tau e_{\tau,2})
\ge F(z-2P)-o(1).
\tag{PR.8}

The pair and residual weights partition the original retained weights occurrence by occurrence, so there is no double counting:

\[
E_{pair}+E_{res}=E_{dom}.
\tag{PR.9}

The selected dominant outer energy is a subbudget of the complete outer detour:

\[
E_{dom}\le\delta+o(1).
\tag{PR.10}

Equations (PR.8)--(PR.10) prove (PR.1).

---

## 2. Low-energy pair extraction with the residual payment retained

Fix an energy threshold `lambda>0`, and let `P_low` be retained pair weight on trains satisfying

\[
e_{\tau,1}+e_{\tau,2}\le\lambda.
\]

Weighted Markov gives

\[
P_{low}\ge P-{E_{pair}\over\lambda}-o(1).
\]

By (PR.1),

\[
\boxed{
P_{low}
\ge
P-{\delta-F(z-2P)\over\lambda}-o(1).
}
\tag{PR.11}

This is the only new quantitative inequality needed below.

It is important that the right side is monotone in the useful direction. Since `0<=z-2P<=pi` and

\[
F'(s)=1-\cos(s/6)\le1-\cos(\pi/6)=1-\sqrt3/2,
\]

we have

\[
{\partial\over\partial P}
\left[
P-{\delta-F(z-2P)\over\lambda}
\right]
=1-{2F'(z-2P)\over\lambda}>0
\tag{PR.12}
\]

whenever

\[
\lambda>2-\sqrt3.
\tag{PR.13}

Thus a lower bound for `P` may safely be substituted into (PR.11).

---

## 3. Pair-weight floor and adverse endpoint reduction

Keep the corrected high-`t` notation

\[
U={9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta-\kappa/2,
\qquad \kappa\ge0.
\tag{PR.14}

Let

\[
U_0(t)={9t\over2}-2\pi.
\]

The dominant-window train mass is at most `U D+o(D)`. Since each train contributes at most one retained occurrence without using pair weight, the standard endpoint-count inequality is

\[
\boxed{
P\ge z-U-o(1)
=z-U_0(t)+2\delta+{\kappa\over2}-o(1).
}
\tag{PR.15}

Substitute the floor in (PR.15) into the right side of (PR.11). For fixed `z,t,delta`, increasing `kappa` increases the pair floor and decreases the residual mass. The derivative is at least

\[
{1\over2}-{F'(s)\over\lambda}>0
\]

for the threshold used below, so the adverse value is `kappa=0`.

With `kappa=0`, put

\[
P_0=z-U_0(t)+2\delta,
\qquad
r_0=z-2P_0=2U_0(t)-z-4\delta.
\tag{PR.16}

The lower-bound functional becomes

\[
L(z,t,\delta)
=P_0-{\delta-F(r_0)\over\lambda}.
\tag{PR.17}

On the live range:

- `L` increases with `z`, because
  \[
  L_z=1-{F'(r_0)\over\lambda}>0;
  \]
- `L` decreases with `delta`, because
  \[
  L_\delta=2-{1\over\lambda}-{4F'(r_0)\over\lambda}<0
  \]
  when `lambda<1/2`;
- `L` decreases with `t`, since `U_0` increases with `t` and
  \[
  {\partial L\over\partial U_0}
  =-1+{2F'(r_0)\over\lambda}<0
  \]
  by (PR.13).

Therefore a uniform endpoint lower bound follows by using

\[
t=T,
\qquad z=z_0,
\qquad\kappa=0,
\qquad\delta=\delta_{max}.
\tag{PR.18}

---

## 4. Corrected retained-mass feasibility at `T=1.865`

Set

\[
T={373\over200}=1.865.
\]

Use the same matched floor as the corrected parent path theorem,

\[
C_{76}=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0076,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\]

Then

\[
U_0={9T\over2}-2\pi,
\qquad
\delta\le\delta_{max}:={U_0-C_{76}\over2}+o(1).
\tag{PR.19}

The exact certificate proves

\[
U_0<2.109315,
\qquad
\boxed{\delta_{max}<0.098545.}
\tag{PR.20}

The corrected retained-mass feasibility system gives

\[
G(\delta,z)\ge-o(1),
\qquad
\delta\ge F(z)-o(1).
\]

On the enlarged endpoint box, the same derivative argument remains valid. The exact verifier checks a uniform lower bound for `G_z`, a lower bound for `G_delta`, `F'(z)<1/16`, and the resulting positivity of

\[
H'(z),
\qquad H(z):=G(F(z),z),
\]

on `0<=z<=2.0738`.

At the endpoint it obtains

\[
\boxed{
H(2.0738)<-7.77\times10^{-5}.
}
\tag{PR.21}

Hence

\[
\boxed{z>2.0738-o(1).}
\tag{PR.22}

---

## 5. The paired remainder leaves `>0.011D` low-energy pair weight

Choose

\[
\boxed{\lambda={2453\over5000}=0.4906.}
\tag{PR.23}

This satisfies

\[
2-\sqrt3<\lambda<{1\over2}.
\]

Use the adverse endpoint values from (PR.18). The exact certificate obtains

\[
P_0>0.1615743,
\tag{PR.24}

\[
r_0=z_0-2P_0>1.75065,
\tag{PR.25}

and the outward bathtub lower bound

\[
F(r_0)>0.0247341.
\tag{PR.26}

Substituting into (PR.17) gives

\[
\boxed{
P_{low}>0.0111250-o(1)>0.011D/D-o(1).
}
\tag{PR.27}

For comparison, the old raw-Markov expression

\[
P_0-\delta_{max}/\lambda
\]

is negative at this endpoint. The entire continuation beyond the old `1.861799` ceiling comes from retaining the residual occurrence payment.

There are at most three dominant phase windows. Therefore one phase carries low-energy pair weight

\[
\boxed{>0.0036-o(1).}
\tag{PR.28}

Since pair weight per train is at most one, that phase contains more than

\[
\boxed{0.0036D-o(D)}
\]

actual O--O complete trains.

---

## 6. Physical opposite branches and the dual path

The parent physical argument applies to every selected train with terminal energy at most `lambda`. Same-oriented terminal branches would force

\[
{r_\tau^2\over D^2}
\le\lambda-{\lambda^2\over4}+o(1),
\]

while Section 157 gives

\[
{r_\tau\over D}
\ge1-\delta-{\lambda\over2}-o(1).
\]

At `delta=delta_max`, the exact certificate proves

\[
\boxed{
\left(1-\delta_{max}-{\lambda\over2}\right)^2
-\left(\lambda-{\lambda^2\over4}\right)
>1.12\times10^{-4}.
}
\tag{PR.29}

Thus every selected train uses opposite oriented terminal branches. Since `lambda<1/2`, the two host arcs remain separated by a fixed positive angular gap.

The same-phase selected O--O crosscuts are pairwise noncrossing and connect the same two disjoint boundary arcs. Therefore their complement dual is one simple path of length

\[
>0.0036D-o(D).
\tag{PR.30}

The phase-free path-saturation theorem makes every path region diameter-loaded. The diameter-path Helly theorem gives one common region on every actual diameter support interval. Every nonhub switch is therefore one-sided toward that hub.

---

## 7. A nonhub strip has at most `1745` switching rows

Let `N_i` be the number of actual diameter intervals starting or stopping at internal path boundary `i`. Every actual diameter interval contributes at most one start and one stop, so

\[
\sum_iN_i\le2|H|\le2\pi D+o(D).
\]

Discard the single hub boundary. More than `0.0036D-o(D)` internal path boundaries remain. Using `pi<355/113`,

\[
{2(355/113)\over0.0036}
=1745.3294\ldots<1746.
\]

Hence some nonhub boundary has integer switch count

\[
\boxed{N_i\le1745.}
\tag{PR.31}

All its switching rows are one-sided toward the hub. The existing strip-reaction nondegeneracy and conic-Caratheodory theorems reduce its exact nonzero layer reaction to one or two actual switching diameter bars.

---

## 8. Frontier effect

The corrected full-core high-`t` normal form is now

```text
1.844 < t <= 1.865:
  retained dominant outer mass z > 2.0738;
  paired O-O weight > 0.1615 D at the adverse endpoint;
  residual unpaired retained mass > 1.7506 D;
  residual bathtub energy > 0.0247 D;
  >0.011 D retained low-energy O-O pair weight;
  >0.0036 D same-phase O-O path trains;
  physical opposite terminal branches;
  every dual path region diameter-loaded;
  one common diameter-routing hub;
  one-sided nonhub switching;
  some nonhub strip has <=1745 moving diameter rows;
  exact reaction generated by <=2 actual bars.
```

A floating-point optimization of the same paired-remainder architecture places its zero-low-mass endpoint near `t=1.865966`. The present theorem deliberately stops at `1.865`, where all decisive inequalities have substantial exact margin.

This still does not close C-II. The remaining closing task is geometric: use the one-sided finite strip and its one/two-bar exact reaction, or strengthen the paired-remainder distribution further.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only paired-remainder extension of the corrected O--O path/hub/one-sided-strip normal form through `t<=1.865`. C-II and Level I remain open.
